#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/id #uuid "3c82270a-33f2-53ee-8876-9d8c7cf8291b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21786" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- $D_1(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+x\\cos(y)\\,y$\n\n- $D_2(y)=\\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\sin(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x}+3y$\n\n- $D_3(y)=\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}}+e^{x}\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}}-\\ln(x)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each $f_i$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator $D_1$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\cos(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator $D_2$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_2(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+x^2}, f_1(x) = -\\sin(x),$ and $f_0(x) = 3.$\n\n- Operator $D_3$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD_3(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y\n$$\n\nwith $f_3(x) = 1, f_2(x) = e^{x}, f_1(x) = 0,$ and $f_0(x) = -\\ln(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228900 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/id #uuid "04f55d6c-825e-587c-8ef4-a5527d1f374e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6569" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L(y_1) = \\tan^2{x}$ and $L(y_2) =2 \\sec^2{x}$ for some functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ What is $L(2y_1 -y_2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\sec^2\\theta = 1+\\tan^2\\theta.$*" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nL(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aL(y_1(x)) + bL(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identity $1+\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x,$ we can simplify this even further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2{tan}^{2}⁡x-2(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nL(2y_1 -y_2)=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228901 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/id #uuid "d01adef6-d3fb-5cd6-b778-5d44fb6702dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6572" :question/problem "Show that the differential operator\n\n$$\nD(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2} + \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nis nonlinear." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(ay_1 + by_2) = aL(y_1) + bL(y_2)\n$$\n\nfor functions $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}})+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & +a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & +ab({y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(ay_1+by_2)  \\neq aD(y_1) + bD(y_2),$ the operator $D$ is nonlinear."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe357496-d00c-5a95-9d7e-a5ab149aed71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{x}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380005} {:answer/id #uuid "e5af527b-172f-5c9e-aa6e-2895c1ee888d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380006} {:answer/id #uuid "dd8d74ed-a05f-5e81-8e14-85796c459364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380007} {:answer/id #uuid "e582321f-ac77-5cf4-9bbb-781a02ed0501" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380008} {:answer/id #uuid "55d138ab-7a12-51db-8e32-158050c97dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8d74ed-a05f-5e81-8e14-85796c459364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380007} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde79d5d-eb70-508a-a325-4360af6cecdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf048097-67b8-5ccb-9658-fc2d3257f9bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-130077" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={e}^{x}+1$ and $L({y}_{2})=2{e}^{x}-3$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2({e}^{x}+1)-(2{e}^{x}-3) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+2-2{e}^{x}+3 \\\\  & =(2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{x})+(2+3) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f771000f-7d71-5e2c-ac7b-d1fc607679c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380012} {:answer/id #uuid "79013f64-57af-5279-b411-bf9955e8997b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380013} {:answer/id #uuid "ad96a296-872d-5a47-87eb-85870a316a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380014} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9982c0-c478-5d0c-b779-de2ed3353e19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380015} {:answer/id #uuid "424ef8a0-36f2-5540-bbbe-197e6fe5f6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{csc}^{2}⁡x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad96a296-872d-5a47-87eb-85870a316a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380014} :answer-field/id #uuid "af16d01b-c365-5c24-81be-600b55e7cf0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad9c70a3-99b4-5fe9-8a82-921436740f17" :question/math-academy-id "q-130079" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={cot}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})=3{csc}^{2}⁡x-4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta =1+{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta .$*" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(4{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =4L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {cot}^{2}⁡x-(3{csc}^{2}⁡x-4) \\\\  & =4{cot}^{2}⁡x+4-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =4(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =4{csc}^{2}⁡x-3{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a21bfd-d5c3-54f6-91d1-2ee32d7c888b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380019} {:answer/id #uuid "c369dc5c-47ad-53fa-bce0-af86b948cf1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380020} {:answer/id #uuid "910c69c9-f1fc-5b56-9790-0e937d68f7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380021} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5e3d4a-1fd5-54c6-82ea-ac7b15e8a59c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380022} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbdea94-e7ed-5a29-9cd8-a8fa9c3ad51d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39a21bfd-d5c3-54f6-91d1-2ee32d7c888b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380019} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ce73186-15ae-5fa4-9822-27c04e5d83ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a8a1c12-7f84-5c3b-83f4-8db057221abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-130256" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nD(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's check each case in turn.\n\n- The first operator is **not** linear. To see why, we first evaluate the left-hand side of the linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{1}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{1}({y}_{2}) & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operator is indeed linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-ax\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-bx\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =a[2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operator is also linear. To see this, we check the above linearity condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{3}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =a{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2ax\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+2bx\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =a[{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}]+b[{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}] \\\\  & =a{D}_{3}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{3}({y}_{2})\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4d658b4-aae8-5c06-8226-eec2bb06a1d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228393} {:answer/id #uuid "d74172d2-57aa-583c-aa0d-f173412474bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228394} {:answer/id #uuid "db7fa18e-f397-51e7-9d51-13cefddd36be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228395} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb195fb-b9f5-5b1e-9fd1-a156600764d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228396} {:answer/id #uuid "57825bd5-751d-54ed-9340-f08f1f292327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57825bd5-751d-54ed-9340-f08f1f292327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228397} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1207603-2b5c-5be1-bba0-e790866e77eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fad9dda-1d37-5d53-a2df-c25ef4515a26" :question/math-academy-id "q-337662" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)={y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}=x,{f}_{1}=-2,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is ${y}^{2},$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The last term contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{2},$ which fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60b937f5-dbbe-5aa6-b2ab-e6fc6de00058" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}\\cdot b{y}_{2})=abL({y}_{1})\\cdot L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340616} {:answer/id #uuid "7f7afe22-4768-5a55-b0b4-735f4e16252d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340617} {:answer/id #uuid "c7506f22-779c-5612-ba66-443191b76f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7506f22-779c-5612-ba66-443191b76f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340618} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc5f13fd-cfa9-55fc-a6f8-befbf797f379" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340615} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33497a63-2ad3-5b92-b633-c40b8613183d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340620} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbec9e-2eef-5a1a-82b5-6a44791ba5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbec9e-2eef-5a1a-82b5-6a44791ba5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340621} :answer-field/id #uuid "05778374-9485-5934-ab5b-f61a0567858f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340619} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027429b5-06bc-5e79-8ba3-e052a387b9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340623} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad2825-8086-58ff-a468-d302df397153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340624} {:answer/id #uuid "6e7b78a4-14b1-5dc7-9652-585647659880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}-{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dad2825-8086-58ff-a468-d302df397153" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340624} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc47255b-c513-5916-b1a0-c2568e81e177" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81cddb1f-17fe-55b8-9ffe-2ace33ea67a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340627} {:answer/id #uuid "680da6a6-423a-5113-b16e-6a605081bb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680da6a6-423a-5113-b16e-6a605081bb7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340628} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efa04f2-e13f-5f23-95e4-521ca4ffc591" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340626} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8878c6d8-f010-5cc2-ae57-e220015cf21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340630} {:answer/id #uuid "26401f57-20a2-525a-97d2-dae744007cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26401f57-20a2-525a-97d2-dae744007cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340631} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bf0fd99-ed7f-512e-9d78-f4942425cd7a" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc0890d7-4776-52c7-95d6-0e2a44d1ab6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340633} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1bc30a-4092-5f24-b647-131c396dcbbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d1bc30a-4092-5f24-b647-131c396dcbbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340634} :answer-field/id #uuid "049f029f-22b5-5205-9f87-6e94a9f62443" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0727572c-0559-503c-b0bd-d0aa08280f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-337728" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +2ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}))}^{2} \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2ab\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & +2ab\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}]+b[\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}] \\\\  & =a\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+b\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d194bb28-8bbf-5b7b-813e-167aa0f64241" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}\\cdot b{y}_{2})=abL({y}_{1})\\cdot L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340637} {:answer/id #uuid "2d086215-3ee5-5fde-b490-82564ba43c9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340638} {:answer/id #uuid "569671fa-f39d-59df-b15d-852bcf0bbbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "569671fa-f39d-59df-b15d-852bcf0bbbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340639} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a78f33-f8cf-557a-b259-52e4fa6b9f31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340636} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baebc494-16b5-51d5-88f6-ae0629bf805f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340641} {:answer/id #uuid "75b021e8-1b55-58c4-a5de-9e6e54d7ad46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75b021e8-1b55-58c4-a5de-9e6e54d7ad46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340642} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4f8c6fd-0294-5f99-bc94-8674893a24b4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340640} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0cd5c28-76e2-58ac-9df3-9299b371727d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340644} {:answer/id #uuid "372b4196-11a6-5da2-83d0-b80f8ab99fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{y}_{1}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0cd5c28-76e2-58ac-9df3-9299b371727d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340644} :answer-field/id #uuid "a934b176-fec5-542b-a7af-29886a60ff09" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b088254f-98c0-5aa3-8098-3e8e7ba49363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340647} {:answer/id #uuid "29913555-7611-5394-9f5a-073bdeeae450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b088254f-98c0-5aa3-8098-3e8e7ba49363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340647} :answer-field/id #uuid "39b22b23-9242-5e16-83ec-018618d95300" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340646} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa9f0f1-cbd6-575b-864f-00ce62d8fbed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340650} {:answer/id #uuid "b61e71d6-87a4-5d34-b9a8-6a426021580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61e71d6-87a4-5d34-b9a8-6a426021580e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340651} :answer-field/id #uuid "73cf32e6-f5f7-5d34-ac5c-4dba8521ca1d" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4cae72f-0477-53ac-8fdb-0b2f5b762c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340653} {:answer/id #uuid "2da4177f-baf3-56e4-9e90-fe534f74ab68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da4177f-baf3-56e4-9e90-fe534f74ab68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654} :answer-field/id #uuid "59ef1d87-7acc-5603-9d7d-2bf5675fd23f" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa9dd2f3-efc6-5afd-a9e5-5871df1e337b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337739" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={{field-2}}+{{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}))}^{2}+(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+2ab\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2} \\\\  & +a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{b}^{2}{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot (2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{y}_{1}]+b[{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+b{(\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}+a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0099fa-c260-5522-8349-b84319f3d9a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340657} {:answer/id #uuid "9e00beba-7ccb-5dcd-a645-6ac3f82084ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340658} {:answer/id #uuid "314ee618-c0ac-565f-8046-5e30b17e6a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340659} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3d95e4-7576-55a1-aad6-1db486808546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} {:answer/id #uuid "38375f01-3694-5244-ab9b-45e80378100b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e00beba-7ccb-5dcd-a645-6ac3f82084ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340658} :answer-field/id #uuid "118e46f1-be4c-5208-998c-6bd38711ed4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42f52e33-d273-52b4-89f9-837138729e09" :question/math-academy-id "q-337765" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=(x-1)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=4\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=x-1$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2x.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=4,{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-{x}^{2},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e68cd434-c89b-5641-91c3-4d7d8918fad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380026} {:answer/id #uuid "6be0c2da-0f70-5671-8152-1a73acbf4a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380027} {:answer/id #uuid "b4bb5587-4929-5e04-bcbc-b3a8a149114e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380028} {:answer/id #uuid "72a4cc06-be9d-5319-a415-e0e3d3a09544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380029} {:answer/id #uuid "e24fa340-907a-52d6-a095-188a1a1ec142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6be0c2da-0f70-5671-8152-1a73acbf4a4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380027} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee8db3a3-3773-5d3d-89aa-6db0b9e17b76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0f47da55-44c0-5b9f-b9f5-4e0250b32c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-337773" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\sqrt{x}y$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)={e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the last term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "640ca961-4afb-5b7b-9a10-0d3e6d27a4e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340664} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee567f6-e9d8-52cc-a654-96427b7f9753" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340665} {:answer/id #uuid "babac767-2c2b-5f88-a4d2-b339a17edf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340666} {:answer/id #uuid "7f28b1a6-0586-5da0-9655-ced3b94a3a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340667} {:answer/id #uuid "11a5e11f-338d-5e83-b87a-41aeb096fde7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11a5e11f-338d-5e83-b87a-41aeb096fde7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340668} :answer-field/id #uuid "52d93a9b-834e-59e5-a570-bee911d26b79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "782599f5-3686-5999-a5ec-95d2e21b628e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337774" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y)\\,y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x),{f}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17c792c0-3aec-5e40-8897-2eab9ff2ece0" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Linear Differential Operators to Identify Them"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "653efa99-e039-53c7-a6d4-6a9dbe7c997f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}\\cdot b{y}_{2})=abL({y}_{1})\\cdot L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380033} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ba0bef-111e-54a3-b3e8-7008c221a9b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380034} {:answer/id #uuid "485ebd57-6ecb-5560-b90b-18d01cb3562d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "485ebd57-6ecb-5560-b90b-18d01cb3562d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "L(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380035} :answer-field/id #uuid "01219c46-bdee-57bf-bb58-9ede70c87223" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380032} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13a84697-a4c0-51c3-a1ba-be028cda83ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380037} {:answer/id #uuid "7a59c87d-3250-52d3-9f6d-6b9dedb3a225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380038} {:answer/id #uuid "714f0393-3e4c-5e2d-9258-e16d501c55df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "714f0393-3e4c-5e2d-9258-e16d501c55df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380039} :answer-field/id #uuid "da4d8e82-b2c0-587f-81a2-ac34578ba6cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea05cdac-0abc-51df-a60d-f77d04c3755c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380041} {:answer/id #uuid "f7aaefb4-542b-58b8-b7c2-a37277672d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({a}^{2}{y}_{1}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea05cdac-0abc-51df-a60d-f77d04c3755c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380041} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d7a4944-0392-5696-bf34-5031ca5829d7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a7acb84-eb88-5362-a700-79a2d4377dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380044} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7d21f5-aa11-5ade-9e00-3b17908d9569" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a7acb84-eb88-5362-a700-79a2d4377dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380044} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f71b01c-f16b-5354-9134-346b2d2685f5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380043} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbc30202-13c6-597d-9ed9-fc499b2fbb07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380047} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb3f247-e070-5f90-9763-d9cee529f24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb3f247-e070-5f90-9763-d9cee529f24f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380048} :answer-field/id #uuid "92002259-14dc-5f74-96ab-44ecfd016fda" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380046} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "458de00b-0d57-5eb4-a5b3-35807c18bb42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "linear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380050} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2a8f23-66a6-5ed9-9ef3-3f3b70fc69e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a2a8f23-66a6-5ed9-9ef3-3f3b70fc69e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "nonlinear" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380051} :answer-field/id #uuid "748285e4-acd7-581b-986c-9e7ab286d242" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05b2756b-6060-5783-bd8d-f31a355170a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-338094" :question/problem "Consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD(y)=y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y\n$$\n\nFill in the steps in the reasoning below.\n\nFor any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={{field-2}}-2\\cdot {{field-3}} \\\\  & +ab\\cdot {{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since {{field-5}} \nthe operator $D$ is {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator $L,$ we have the linearity condition\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=aL({y}_{1})+bL({y}_{2})\n$$\n\nfor functions ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and where $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nFor the given differential operator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x})-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2a{y}_{1}-2b{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2\\cdot (a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & +ab\\cdot ({y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\ .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as the right-hand side, because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}) & =a[{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{1}]+b[{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{2}] \\\\  & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}-2(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $D(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\ne aD({y}_{1})+bD({y}_{2}),$ the operator $D$ is $\\text{nonlinear}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3acd6d67-b727-518e-b03a-c63727d51748" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340685} {:answer/id #uuid "6a49c2fb-acad-5e7d-a7f2-9f5e5c261a7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340686} {:answer/id #uuid "668d26ca-9205-5010-a644-31a644074490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340687} {:answer/id #uuid "1292c7b1-f809-555d-a22f-9f83d1c06cfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340688} {:answer/id #uuid "73b2bb90-c77c-5838-ad26-8497bcaf7d08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "668d26ca-9205-5010-a644-31a644074490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340687} :answer-field/id #uuid "65a203b6-2565-590b-8ce9-8b4e023944f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b82a08be-c14d-5d29-a634-0558fbfaadff" :question/math-academy-id "q-338104" :question/problem "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\n- ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n- ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y)\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y$\n- ${D}_{3}(y)=(1+{x}^{2})\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}y$" :question/worked-solution "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is $x\\operatorname{tan}⁡(y),$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=1+{x}^{2},{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186340690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d4f52f-fb16-50dd-b967-ddb1d1580aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340692} {:answer/id #uuid "8995a31d-bd20-5ffe-a31c-459c3295c4b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340693} {:answer/id #uuid "2371e7fb-d44f-5656-b5ed-6ed804253cf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340694} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0e5ad3-9790-5341-aafa-3f8153ac9a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340695} {:answer/id #uuid "cb7beb16-c2d7-50c0-900e-a455b82ed4eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d4f52f-fb16-50dd-b967-ddb1d1580aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340692} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1e0702c-75a2-5b2d-b62d-d9738c350e8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e2de42e-6e49-52a4-9c7d-88b4b8bf3ab1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64900" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=2{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and $L({y}_{2})={sin}^{2}⁡x+4$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}+2{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})+2L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2\\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡x+4) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x+8 \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)+8 \\\\  & =2+8 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e826d53b-c248-5e02-81cd-6ffe560c28cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x + 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228648} {:answer/id #uuid "12975fa0-b12b-59b8-a74b-9789c9da9fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x^{2} + 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228649} {:answer/id #uuid "333ab26d-cbe8-54eb-b402-2e88f3323434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (x - 2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228650} {:answer/id #uuid "6d760258-c364-513c-b1b6-7f1e2817d8e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228651} {:answer/id #uuid "7a5002a4-3b44-5200-a54b-12bf2eba4c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d760258-c364-513c-b1b6-7f1e2817d8e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x - 1)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228651} :answer-field/id #uuid "75d97097-784a-5900-a91f-b784925cbe64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18ff6e02-5e97-55df-8602-9a9eb6b62402" :question/math-academy-id "q-65035" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=4x$ and $L({y}_{2})={x}^{2}+1$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}-2{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})-2L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =4x-2({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{2}+4x-2 \\\\  & =-2({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-2(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" :knowledge-point/title "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef50d35e-0b69-5ec6-8a90-4de29a7ed011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380054} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9cf061-7f5e-55c8-af91-7692f3c4e70a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380055} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a3ace8-17ed-57a9-9e8b-06e21d220cd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380056} {:answer/id #uuid "c33b5e08-82ba-5fdb-aeb7-3514f60cd69b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380057} {:answer/id #uuid "3dfb56cd-9d37-512e-8110-174346f5691b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380058}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef50d35e-0b69-5ec6-8a90-4de29a7ed011" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380054} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f11b98-86d4-5ab2-aa5b-2f2eda2a46ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e7a3c2e-9c63-59a7-997e-a36d48877090" :question/math-academy-id "q-79606" :question/problem "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={x}^{2}+3x+5$ and $L({y}_{2})=x+2$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L({y}_{1}-3{y}_{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L({y}_{1}-3{y}_{2}) & =L({y}_{1})-3L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5-3(x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+3x+5-3x-6 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
